# CLI conventions (`@executioncontrolprotocol/cli`)

## User-facing tokens are kebab-case

Anything a user types as a name follows **kebab-case**, the same family as enum and union
literals in `typescript.mdc`. TypeScript field names stay camelCase; the CLI is a separate
surface.

- **Long flags:** kebab-case (`--default-model`, `--sort-field`). Never camelCase or
  snake_case.
- **Generated flags:** `plugin-sdk` derives flag names from input schema field names by
  kebab-casing them (`maxCostUsd` → `--max-cost-usd`). Don't hand-name generated flags.
- **Subcommand and topic segments:** kebab-case (`ecp format-toon encode`,
  `ecp config secrets add`).
- **Closed-set flag values:** use the same kebab-case literals as the type union.
- **Short flags:** single letters, only where clearly useful, and never colliding with oclif's
  own (`-h`).

Free-form values (paths, ids, URLs, secrets) are not renamed.

## Verbs

Use the same vocabulary everywhere:

| Verb | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| `list` | Many items (`ecp runs list`, `ecp config secrets list`) |
| `show` | One item in detail (`ecp runs show <id>`) |
| `add` / `remove` | Create / delete an entry (`ecp config secrets add`) |
| `update` | Change an existing entry, or update installed things (`ecp update`) |
| Domain verbs | Actions that aren't CRUD: `run`, `resume`, `rerun`, `validate`, `compile`, `invoke`, `search`, `describe`, `discover`, `install`, `init`, `new`, `build`, `check` |

The `ecp plugins` topic comes from `@oclif/plugin-plugins` and keeps its own verbs (`install`,
`uninstall`, `link`, `inspect`, `reset`, `update`). Our authoring commands in that topic are
`new`, `build` and `check`.

## How commands are defined

- Every command is generated. The CLI's own commands are `defineOperation(...)`
  definitions (zod input, metadata, handler, optional `cli: { args: [...] }` for positional
  arguments) turned into commands by `toCommands()`.
- Plugin commands come from extension definitions (`ecp plugins build`).
- No hand-written oclif command classes. The only hand-written oclif code is the CLI's hooks.
- **`--json`:** every command supports it, printing the envelope `{ ok, data }` or
  `{ ok: false, error: { code, message, path, suggestions } }`. Errors use `ECP_*` codes and
  the exit codes in `RESET-PLAN.md` §4.6. `EcpCommand` owns this, so oclif's own JSON error
  output never leaks.
- **Reserved root names** that plugins can't use as topics: `init`, `search`, `describe`,
  `validate`, `install`, `run`, `runs`, `invoke`, `compile`, `config`, `discover`, `update`,
  `rollback`, `plugin`, `plugins`, `mcp`, `app`, `help`.

## `--env`

`--env <file>` is an optional environment overlay. `ecp run` / `validate` / `install` run a
workflow file against the process host when the flag is omitted. The file is
`environment(id).with([...])`, not a required workflow+environment pair.

## Run, events, and journals

- `ecp run <file> --to <step>` stops after that leaf step with status `stopped`.
- `ecp run --events` streams NDJSON (`run.start`, `step.start`, `step.policy`, `step.end`,
  `step.error`, `run.end`). The last line is `run.end`.
- `ecp runs list|show|resume|rerun` inspect and continue journals under `.ecp/runs/`.
  `resume --approve` records an approval for a paused step. `rerun --from` / `--to` reuse
  earlier journal outputs. There is no `ecp test`.

## One path per concern

- Don't add parallel command trees for the same thing (for example `extensions` and `plugins`).
- Rename in place when terminology changes.

## `ecp config secrets`

`ecp config secrets add KEY` (interactive) or `add KEY --value …`; `get KEY`, `remove KEY`,
`list`.
